AO_HABILIDAD_ControlPresurizador;Manage Pressurizer
AO_HABILIDAD_D_ControlPresurizador;The <b>Pressurizer</b> is necessary to prevent hot water from boiling inside the core or primary circuit.<br><br>For the cost of <sprite=0>{0} Prestige Points per hour, AO will manage the Pressurizer for you.
AO_HABILIDAD_ControlBarrasDeControl;Manage Control Rods
AO_HABILIDAD_D_ControlBarrasDeControl;AO will stabilize the core by adjusting rods at the cost of <sprite=0>{0} Prestige Points per hour.
AO_HABILIDAD_ParadaDeEmergencia;Emergency Stop
AO_HABILIDAD_D_ParadaDeEmergencia;AO will safely shut down the reactor.
AO_HABILIDAD_TareasIlimitadas;Perform Unlimited Maintenance
AO_HABILIDAD_D_TareasIlimitadas;In most cases, AO can accept only up to five (5) simultaneous tasks.<br><br>After this upgrade, AO will handle unlimited simultaneous tasks until its battery runs out.
AO_HABILIDAD_ControlTotal;Complete control of the reactor.
AO_HABILIDAD_D_ControlTotal;Complete control of the reactor.
AO_HABILIDAD_RecargaCombustibleElectrogenos;Refuel Diesel Generators
AO_HABILIDAD_D_RecargaCombustibleElectrogenos;The <b>Diesel Generator</b> can provide power for days; however, it must be manually refueled when running low.<br><br>For the cost of <sprite=0>{0} Prestige Points, AO can monitor and refill the Diesel Generator fuel tanks as needed.
AO_HABILIDAD_Hal9000;Special mode by scenario award.
AO_HABILIDAD_D_Hal9000;Special mode by scenario award.
T_AYUDA;GAMEPLAY HELP
T_AY_00;Active Alarms
T_AY_01;Reactor start-up protocol
T_AY_02;Stabilization Protocol
T_AY_03;Reactor shutdown protocol
T_AY_04;Analyze situation for reactor shutdown protocol
T_AY_99;BACK TO PREVIOUS MENU
T_AYUDA_UI;HOTKEYS
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_0;Internal emergency power generation.
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_0;The internal power supply is necessary to operate the plant during the start-up protocol, both to power the modules and controls, and to operate the machines and circulation pumps.<br><br><b>It is not recommended to start the protocol with only emergency batteries.</b>
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_1;Activate and energize the reactor modules and the <b>Control Room</b> modules.
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_1;The Control Room and the control modules must be active and energized:<br> Core, Fuel, Control Rods, Coolant, Generator Turbines, Pressurizer, Evaporators.<br><br><br>If the internal energy is not enough to energize all the critical modules for the operation of the plant, you should review the consumption and deactivate those that are not critical.<br><br>The control room is the point in a power plant where all of the information required for its efficient control and management converges.
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_2;Ensure the correct volume of coolant inside the cooling circuit.
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_2;The cooling circuit comprises the core reactor vessel, pressurizer, circulation pumps, steam generators, and condensers.<br>If the coolant volume is not correct within the entire system, it will not be possible to operate the reactor.<br>Use the charge pump and adjust the valves to deliver coolant to the circuit.<br><br>The heat released by fission in nuclear reactors must be captured and transferred for use in electricity generation.<br>
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_3;Stabilize the coolant circulation inside the <b>Reactor Core.</b>
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_3;Activate the reactor core coolant circulation pumps, <b>set their speed to 50%</b>, and wait until the speed has stabilized.<br>If the coolant begins to circulate correctly, the core flow indicators will show the variations.<br>Verify that the pumps have the associated valves open.<br>There may be one or more circulation pumps in the circuit.<br><br>Reactors use coolants that remove heat from the core where the fuel is processed and carry it to electrical generators.<br>Coolants also serve to maintain manageable pressures within the core.
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_4;Stabilize the circulation of the coolant from the generation room <b>Condensers.</b>
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_4;Activate the condenser circulation pumps, set their speed to 50%, and wait until the speed has stabilized.<br>Verify that the pumps have the associated valves open.<br>There may be one or more circulation pumps in the circuit.<br><br>A <b>Condenser</b> is a large heat exchanger designed to cool exhaust steam from a turbine below the boiling point so that it can be returned to the heat source as water.
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_5;Stabilize the circulation of the coolant towards the <b>Cooling Towers.</b>
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_5;Activate the cooling tower circulation pumps, set their speed to 50%, and wait until the speed has stabilized.<br>Verify that the pumps have the associated valves open.<br><br>Cooling towers provide an energy efficient and environmentally friendly way of removing heat from this circulating water before it is returned to its source.
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_6;Prepare the reactor core <b>Pressurizer.</b>
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_6;The water circulates in a liquid state and at very high pressure through the primary circuit, through the pumps that propel it through the Reactor Core, where it is heated before being directed to the steam generators.<br>The <b>Pressurizer</b> has its own heater, which allows it to reach the optimum temperature and pressure.<br>Verify that the pressure and temperature of the pressurizer, and of the entire primary circuit, are within the operating parameters.
T_AY_PE0_CORTO_7;Introduce the <b>Control Rods</b> at 100% inside the Reactor Core.
T_AY_PE0_EXTENDIDO_7;A control rod is a device used to absorb neutrons so that the nuclear chain reaction taking place in the reactor core can be slowed down or stopped altogether by inserting the rods further in, or speed up by withdrawing them slightly.<br>To start the ignition protocol, the <b>control rods</b> must be inserted 100% inside the Reactor Core.<br>
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_0;Introduce the fuel cell inside the Reactor Core.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_0;In order for fission to be initiated, the radioactive fuel cell must be introduced into the reactor core.<br>Note that when you insert the fuel cell, the chain reaction will start immediately.<br><br>In nuclear fission, atoms are split apart, which releases energy.<br>A neutron collides with a uranium atom and splits it, releasing a large amount of energy in the form of heat and radiation.
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_1;Extract the <b>Control Rods</b> until the reaction starts.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_1;To initiate fission, progressively pull out the control rods until the core has reached critical mass.<br><b>Attention:</b> If the fuel cell is new and the control rods are removed too quickly, a rapid reaction can occur, compromising the integrity of the reactor core.<br><br>The critical mass of a fission reactor is the mass of fissile material required to maintain a chain reaction.<br>
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_2;Stabilize the <b>Reactor Core</b> at operating temperature.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_2;Once the Core has reached <b>critical mass</b>, adjust the position of the control rods until the core temperature reaches and stays within the operating range.<br><br>If the mass of a reaction surpasses its critical mass, the result is an uncontrolled chain reaction that culminates in a large explosion (this is how an atomic bomb works).
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_3;Stabilize power generation from the turbines.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_3;Once the core has reached critical mass and has been stabilized within the operating range, verify that the generating turbines are generating power within the operating range.<br>You can regulate circulation pumps to balance core temperature and power generation.<br><br>Nuclear chain reactions produce heat through a physical process called fission.<br>The heat produced during nuclear fission in the reactor core is used to boil water into steam, which turns the blades of a steam turbine.<br>As the turbine blades turn, they drive generators that make electricity.
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_4;Provide energy to the external network.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_4;The energy generated by the electric turbines must be supplied to the external network.<br>The daily service level requirements are measured according to the energy delivered to the external network.<br>Internal consumption is not taken into account.
T_AY_PE1_CORTO_5;Shut off internal emergency power.
T_AY_PE1_EXTENDIDO_5;The reactor will be fully stabilized when it does not depend on any internal source of energy, be it diesel generators or emergency batteries.<br>Verify that the diesel generators are not running, and the emergency batteries are not delivering power to the internal circuits.<br><br>Backup electricity supply systems are required to provide cooling for decay heat produced in shut-down reactors and to maintain services (e.g., system control, lighting, communication, and ventilation).<br>
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_0;Turn on at least one diesel generator or verify that there are charged emergency batteries.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_0;The turbines will stop generating energy, therefore, before starting the shutdown procedure, verify that there is at least one diesel generator ready to work, or emergency batteries with sufficient charge to maintain the plant during the shutdown and cooling process.
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_1;Introduce the <b>Control Rods</b> at 100% inside the Reactor Core.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_1;A control rod is a device used to absorb neutrons so that the nuclear chain reaction taking place in the reactor core can be slowed down or stopped altogether by inserting the rods further in, or speed up by withdrawing them slightly.<br>To start the shutdown protocol, the <b>control rods</b> must be inserted 100% inside the Reactor Core.<br>
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_2;Activate the coolant charge pump of the condensation circuit.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_2;During the cooling procedure, the coolant from the condensation circuit will be used to help lower the temperature of the main circuit of the reactor core.<br>For this reason, the condenser must maintain a volume much higher than under normal operating levels.
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_3;Activate the circulation pump of the condenser coolant circuit.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_3;Cooling pumps draw in external water and circulate it through the condenser to cool it.
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_4;Activate the coolant circulation pumps between the condenser and the evaporators.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_4;The condenser/evaporator circulation pumps draw coolant from the condenser and propel it to the evaporators to cool them.
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_5;Opening of the evaporator drain valve if the temperature is too high or if it is necessary to reduce it more quickly.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_5;To reduce the temperature of the evaporators, it is recommended to open the drain valve, but it must be controlled with extreme caution that coolant is not lost faster than it is received from the condensing circuit, since it could affect the general cooling of the primary circuit.
T_AY_PE2_CORTO_6;Fuel cell removal.
T_AY_PE2_EXTENDIDO_6;Once the core has been sufficiently cooled, the fuel cell can be removed, ending the shutdown protocol.
T_AY_04_TITULO;Reactor shutdown protocol
T_AY_04_SUBTITULO;Analyze situation
T_AY_04_DESCRIPCION;Shutdown can be as complex as startup.<br>Be aware that the reactor may prevent the shutdown from being slower than normal, and may even prevent an emergency maneuver from being initiated.<br><br>For these reasons, it is important to carry out a complete analysis of the situation, to know the different elements and situations that may be complicating the shutdown.
T_AY_04_DESCRIPCION_RESUMIDA;Shutdown can be as complex as startup.<br>There are several considerations to take into account.
T_PANEL_ALARMAS;Annunciator Panel
D_PANEL_ALARMAS;To acknowledge the alarm, press the <color=blue>SILENCE ALARMS</color> button in the middle of the control panel. The light will stop blinking and remain on.
